                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "In Extremis is a strange little shoot em up that feels like it is trying to do more than just throw enemies and bullets at me. It is a fast 2D space shooter with a very strong psychedelic style, but each stage also has its own idea and atmosphere. I am moving through eleven stages that cover themes like war, fear, nostalgia, melancholy and sex, and the game keeps changing how those stages look and play. I like that because I never feel completely sure what the next section is going to do with the basic shoot em up formula.\n\nThe biggest strength for me is the variety between the stages. Each one has its own visual style and different mechanics, so the game does not simply repeat the same type of level with a different background. There are also twelve weapons to experiment with, and they have their own quirks rather than all feeling like slightly different versions of the same gun. Some sections are much more focused on dodging and positioning, while others give me different things to think about. That keeps the short campaign moving at a good pace.\n\nThe soundtrack is another part I really like. It jumps between very different styles, including jazz, electronic music, piano and psychedelic rock, which fits the strange atmosphere surprisingly well. The visuals are also deliberately unusual, and I get the feeling that a lot of the enemies, bosses and environments are meant to represent something rather than simply look cool. There is a mysterious story running underneath everything too, with secrets and hidden details that give me a reason to pay attention instead of just concentrating on surviving the next wave.\n\nThe actual shooting is solid, although I think the game is much more interesting because of everything surrounding it. The controls are simple enough for me to understand quickly, but the difficulty can become pretty demanding once the bullet patterns start getting heavier. I have to learn where to move, when to attack and which weapon works best for the situation. It has that arcade feeling where dying does not necessarily feel like the end of the experience because I can immediately understand what I did wrong and try again.\n\nThe main weakness for me is the length. There are eleven stages and a lot of variety packed into them, but the whole experience is still fairly short. That works when I want a quick arcade style game, but it also means I do not get a huge amount of content for the amount of ideas being introduced. Some of the unusual mechanics also take a little while to understand, and I think the experimental approach will not work equally well for me in every stage. The game is more interested in trying something different than building one perfectly consistent shooting system.\n\nI also think the presentation can sometimes be a little too strange for its own good. I like the psychedelic style and the symbolic ideas, but there are moments where I am more interested in working out what I am looking at than actually focusing on the gameplay. That is not necessarily a bad thing, because the developer clearly wanted the game to feel unusual, but it can make some sections less immediately enjoyable. Still, I appreciate the ambition much more than I would appreciate another generic space shooter that played it completely safe.\n\nOverall, In Extremis is a creative and surprisingly memorable shoot em up. I like the eleven different stages, unusual mechanics, twelve weapons, strange soundtrack and experimental presentation, and I think the game does a good job making each section feel like its own little experience. The short length and occasional confusion hold it back, and the unusual design will not always work perfectly for me, but there is enough personality here to make it stand out. It is also completely free, which makes it much easier for me to recommend when I can simply try it and see whether the strange style works for me.\n\nRating: 7.9/10\n",
